      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we need to convert both logarithms to a common base or express them in terms of base 10 or base \\(e\\), or use properties of logarithms. However, we can start by expressing \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in a simpler form.\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) can be rewritten using the change of base formula or directly by expressing 27 as a power of 3. We know that \\(27 = 3^3\\), so we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = \\frac{\\log_3 3}{\\log_3 3^3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis equation tells us that \\(x\\) raised to the power of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) is equal to 4. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[\nx^{1/3} = 4\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we raise both sides of the equation to the power of 3:\n\\[\n(x^{1/3})^3 = 4^3\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
