      "text": "To find \\( x \\) such that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we will use properties of logarithms and exponents. Let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in a more manageable form. We know that \\(27 = 3^3\\), so we can rewrite the logarithm as:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3\n\\]\nUsing the change of base formula for logarithms, \\(\\log_{a^b} c = \\frac{1}{b} \\log_a c\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{3^3} 3 = \\frac{1}{3} \\log_3 3\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_3 3 = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis equation tells us that \\(x\\) raised to the power of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) equals 4. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[\nx^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we cube both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n\\left( x^{\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)^3 = 4^3\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
