      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Starting with the given equation $\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3$, we can use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that $\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}$ for any positive $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\nApplying the change of base formula to both sides of the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nSince $\\log x$ appears in both denominators, we can simplify $\\log_{27} 3$ as follows:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log (3^3)} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSolving for $\\log x$, we get:\n\\[\n\\log 4 = \\frac{\\log x}{3} \\implies \\log x = 3 \\log 4\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that $a \\log b = \\log b^a$, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log x = \\log 4^3 \\implies \\log x = \\log 64\n\\]\n\nSince the logarithms are equal, their arguments must be equal:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
